[candidate · 0:41] On the math: COGS is 58% of revenue now versus 42% before. If I hold the dollar cost of inputs fixed and raise prices by x, COGS as a percent of revenue falls by a factor of 1/(1+x). To get 58% back down to roughly 48% I'd need 58/(1+x) = 48, so 1+x ≈ 1.21 — call it 20%, which is higher than I said. To recover about half the gap, down to 53%, that's 58/53 ≈ 1.09, so about 9%. That's where the high-single-digit to low-teens range comes from, and I should have been clearer it only recovers part of the gap, not most of it.

On the exhibit — the baseline row is the anchor: two years ago COGS was 42% and profit 24%. That's the healthy state we're trying to get back to, and it tells me a 42% COGS structure supports a 24% margin. So pricing isn't the whole story; we'd also need to pull COGS itself down toward that 42% through supplier renegotiation or hedging beans.
